---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { VERSION, LIMITS } from '../schema.ts';
import { listPresets } from '../presets.ts';
---
<Layout title="Documentation · Microworld">
<main id="main" class="docs-shell page-width">
<aside class="docs-nav" aria-label="Documentation sections">
<strong>Build with Microworld</strong>
<a href="#quickstart">Quickstart</a>
<a href="#coordinates">Coordinates & chunks</a>
<a href="#response">World data</a>
<a href="#presets">Presets & terrain</a>
<a href="#placement">Placement rules</a>
<a href="#templates">Tile templates</a>
<a href="#mcp">MCP</a>
<a href="#limits">Limits & errors</a>
<a href="#reproducibility">Reproducibility</a>
</aside>
<article class="docs-content">
<span class="eyebrow">DOCUMENTATION / GENERATOR {VERSION}</span>
<h1>A world to build on.</h1>
<p class="lead">Microworld generates initial world data. You decide how to render it, what players can do, and where to store their changes.</p>
<section id="quickstart">
<h2>Your first world</h2>
<p>Send a <code>QUERY</code> request with a JSON body. No account or API key is required.</p><pre><code>{`curl --request QUERY \
  https://microworld.kaf.sh/api/world \
  --header 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  --data '{
    "generatorVersion": "${VERSION}",
    "seed": "my-first-world",
    "preset": "overworld",
    "window": { "x": 0, "y": 0, "width": 32, "height": 32 }
  }'`}</code></pre><p>Or request a window from your browser or game client:</p><pre><code>{`const response = await fetch("https://microworld.kaf.sh/api/world", {
  method: "QUERY",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    generatorVersion: "${VERSION}",
    seed: "my-first-world",
    preset: "overworld",
    window: { x: 0, y: 0, width: 32, height: 32 },
  }),
});
if (!response.ok) throw new Error(await response.text());
const world = await response.json();`}</code></pre></section>
<section id="coordinates">
<h2>One world, many windows</h2>
<p>World coordinates are nonnegative safe integers. Each axis contains <code>Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER</code> cells, so the final cell coordinate is <code>9007199254740990</code>. The window must fit entirely within the world.</p>
<p>A chunk is 16 × 16 cells. To fetch chunk <code>cx, cy</code>, request <code>x: cx * 16</code>, <code>y: cy * 16</code>, <code>width: 16</code>, and <code>height: 16</code>. Windows need not align with chunks. Request several chunks together to reduce HTTP overhead.</p>
<div class="docs-note">
<p>The playground pans inside its loaded window. A game that needs to keep exploring should request neighboring windows as the player moves and cache them locally.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="response">
<h2>Read the world data</h2>
<p>Elevation, biome, and walkability arrays use row-major order. The array index for a local cell is <code>y * width + x</code>.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>
<code>generatorVersion</code>
</td>
<td>The algorithm version used for this response.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<code>seed</code>, <code>preset</code>, <code>window</code>
</td>
<td>The requested world identity and window.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<code>configuration</code>
</td>
<td>Resolved terrain settings and placement rules.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<code>elevation</code>
</td>
<td>One integer per cell, between 0 and 65535. Relative height, not meters.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<code>biome</code>
</td>
<td>One biome identifier per cell, such as <code>forest</code> or <code>ocean</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<code>objects</code>
</td>
<td>Placed objects intersecting the window, with world coordinates and footprints.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<code>tiles</code>
</td>
<td>Nonempty template cells inside the requested window, with their owning object IDs.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table><pre><code>{`for (let y = 0; y < world.window.height; y++) {
  for (let x = 0; x < world.window.width; x++) {
    const i = y * world.window.width + x;
    drawCell(x, y, world.biome[i], world.elevation[i]);
  }
}`}</code></pre><p>Object origins can lie outside the requested window when their footprints cross its edge. Deduplicate objects by ID when combining windows.</p>
</section>
<section id="presets">
<h2>Start somewhere interesting</h2>
<p>Presets supply terrain and placement defaults. Override individual terrain fields. A supplied <code>placements</code> array replaces the preset's placement rules; use an empty array to disable objects.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Preset</th>
<th>World</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>{listPresets().map(p=>
<tr>
<td>
<code>{p.id}</code>
</td>
<td>{p.description}</td>
</tr>)}</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Terrain controls</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Setting</th>
<th>Range</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>
<code>scale</code>
</td>
<td>4–4096</td>
<td>Size of terrain features in cells.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<code>seaLevel</code>
</td>
<td>0–65535</td>
<td>Water threshold for terrain presets.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<code>roughness</code>
</td>
<td>0–1</td>
<td>Strength of smaller terrain details.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<code>moisture</code>
</td>
<td>0–1</td>
<td>Climate moisture bias.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<code>temperature</code>
</td>
<td>0–1</td>
<td>Climate temperature bias.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Structured presets such as chess, arena, and maze define their own geometry. Not every terrain control changes every preset.</p><pre><code>{`{
  "seed": "archipelago",
  "preset": "islands",
  "terrain": { "scale": 64, "seaLevel": 32000 },
  "placements": [],
  "window": { "x": 0, "y": 0, "width": 128, "height": 128 }
}`}</code></pre></section>
<section id="walking"><h2>Put a character in the world</h2>
<p>The response includes <code>walkable</code>, a boolean array aligned with the terrain. Check <code>world.walkable[y * width + x]</code> before moving a character into a local cell. Water, mountain peaks, walls, trees, and other solid objects block movement. Doors, floors, and roads allow it. Custom tile identifiers default to walkable, so apply your own rules when adding new tile types.</p>
<p>Try <strong>Walk around</strong> in the playground. Arrow keys, WASD, and the on-screen controls move the character using this same data. Choose <strong>Classic RPG</strong> for terrain tiles inspired by early console RPGs, or <strong>Block map</strong> for a top-down block-world view.</p></section>
<section id="custom-biomes">
<h2>Name your own biomes</h2>
<p>Pass a <code>biomes</code> array to classify terrain using your own identifiers. Each definition can constrain elevation, moisture, and temperature. Rules use the first matching custom definition; cells that do not match keep their preset biome. Mark water biomes with <code>water: true</code> so placement proximity rules recognize them.</p>
<pre><code>{`"biomes": [
  { "id": "silver-lake", "maxElevation": 28000, "water": true },
  { "id": "enchanted-woods", "minMoisture": 0.6 },
  { "id": "meadow" }
]`}</code></pre>
<p>Use the same identifiers in placement rules and in your renderer. Custom biome rules apply to structured presets too, so leave them out when you want to retain a chessboard or maze's built-in labels.</p>
</section>
<section id="placement">
<h2>Put things in their place</h2>
<p>Rules describe candidates on a deterministic placement grid. A candidate appears only when it passes its probability, biome, elevation, and spacing checks. A placement request never guarantees an exact object count.</p><pre><code>{`{
  "seed": "woodland",
  "preset": "overworld",
  "placements": [{
    "id": "trees",
    "object": "oak",
    "pitch": 12,
    "probability": 0.7,
    "minSpacing": 3,
    "biomes": ["forest", "grassland"],
    "minElevation": 29000,
    "maxElevation": 45000,
    "maxRelief": 3000
  }],
  "window": { "x": 0, "y": 0, "width": 64, "height": 64 }
}`}</code></pre><p>
<code>priority</code> ranges from 0 to 100 and defaults to 0. Higher priorities reserve space before lower ones, so buildings can take precedence over trees. <code>pitch</code> sets the candidate grid spacing. <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> define footprints. <code>maxRelief</code> limits the elevation difference across a footprint. <code>nearWater</code> limits distance to water, up to 16 cells. See the <a href="https://github.com/iamkaf/microworld/blob/main/docs/api-contract.md">full API contract</a> for collision priority, spacing semantics, and all validation rules.</p>
</section>
<section id="templates">
<h2>Give structures some substance</h2>
<p>Use a built-in template such as <code>cottage</code> or <code>ruin</code>, or pass a rectangular array of tile names. A <code>null</code> cell emits no tile. Inline template dimensions must match the footprint.</p><pre><code>{`{
  "id": "shelters",
  "object": "shelter",
  "pitch": 32,
  "width": 3,
  "height": 3,
  "biomes": ["grassland"],
  "rotations": [0, 90, 180, 270],
  "template": [
    ["wall", "wall", "wall"],
    ["wall", "floor", "wall"],
    ["wall", "door", "wall"]
  ]
}`}</code></pre><p>Templates provide tile identifiers, not textures or procedural interiors. Your renderer maps each identifier to an image, color, or game entity. The server clips emitted tiles to the requested window while preserving the full object's identity.</p>
</section>
<section id="mcp">
<h2>Give your agent a world</h2>
<p>The stateless MCP endpoint is <code>https://microworld.kaf.sh/mcp</code>. It targets the MCP 2026-07-28 specification. Use a client that supports that protocol version and its stateless HTTP transport.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tool</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>
<code>list_presets</code>
</td>
<td>Discover the preset library.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<code>get_preset</code>
</td>
<td>Read a preset's defaults and customization options.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<code>generate_world</code>
</td>
<td>Generate a window with the same inputs as the QUERY API.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Try asking your agent: "Show me Microworld's presets, then generate a 32 × 32 island world with seed driftwood." The API and MCP share the same generator and validation. No session or account is required.</p>
<p>See the <a href="https://github.com/iamkaf/microworld/blob/main/docs/api-contract.md">transport contract and wire examples</a> for the exact request headers and JSON-RPC messages.</p>
</section>
<section id="limits">
<h2>Keep requests bounded</h2>
<p>The default limits are {LIMITS.maxSide} cells per side, {LIMITS.maxCells.toLocaleString('en-US')} cells per window, and {LIMITS.maxRules} placement rules. The hosted service may enforce lower limits. Complex placement rules can reach the work budget before the cell limit.</p>
<p>Always check the HTTP status before reading world data. Invalid inputs, oversized requests, and generation work limits produce errors. Reduce your window or simplify placements when a request exceeds the budget. Avoid retrying invalid requests unchanged.</p>
<p>Public hosting has finite capacity. Cache generated windows, generate on demand, and use your own deployment if your game needs dedicated capacity.</p>
</section>
<section id="reproducibility">
<h2>Keep your world yours</h2>
<p>Save the seed, generator version, preset, and configuration alongside your game. Pin <code>generatorVersion: "{VERSION}"</code> when reproducibility matters. Omitting the version selects the server's default.</p>
<p>Microworld generates the initial state only. Store cut trees, opened doors, moved pieces, and player buildings in your own persistence layer, then apply them over the generated world.</p>
<p>Microworld is <a href="https://github.com/iamkaf/microworld">open source under Apache 2.0</a>. You can inspect the implementation, contribute presets, or run your own Worker.</p>
</section>
</article>
</main>
</Layout>
